    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hebrew, a Canaanite language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> kingdoms of Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, is the language of most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, written and edited over many centuries in the 1st millennium BCE.</li><li>Displaced in daily life by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, Greek and later other languages, it lived on for nearly two thousand years as the language of Jewish prayer, law, poetry, philosophy and correspondence across the world.</li><li>Revived as a spoken language from the late 19th century, it is today the language of Israel and of some 9 million speakers, a unique case of a language reborn as the mother tongue of a nation.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Ben-Zion Ben-Yehuda, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1882 and raised by his father Eliezer to speak only Hebrew, is often called the first native speaker of the language in modern times.</p></aside>
    </section>

