    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/siloam-tunnel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siloam-tunnel.jpg" alt="Siloam tunnel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/siloam-tunnel">Siloam tunnel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siloam22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tamar Hayardeni תמר הירדני, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hebrew belongs to the Canaanite group of Northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>, together with Phoenician, Moabite, Ammonite and Edomite, all of them closely related dialects spoken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. The Bible calls it “the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>” and yehudit, “Judaean”; the name Hebrew, ivrit, came into use later.</p><p>The earliest inscriptions in Hebrew or closely related dialects date from around the 10th century BCE. The Gezer calendar, a small limestone tablet listing the months of the agricultural year, and an ostracon from Khirbet Qeiyafa, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, are among them, though scholars debate whether their language is Hebrew or another Canaanite dialect. From the 9th and 8th centuries BCE, inscriptions are clearly Hebrew: the administrative ostraca of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, capital of the northern kingdom of Israel, and the Siloam inscription, carved around 700 BCE in a tunnel dug to bring water into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, which describes the moment when the two teams of diggers met.</p><p>Two small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> amulets found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ketef-hinnom">Ketef Hinnom</a> in Jerusalem, from around 600 BCE, bear a version of the Priestly Blessing from the Book of Numbers, the oldest known text also found in the Bible. The Lachish letters, written on potsherds by officers of Judah as the Babylonian army advanced in 588 or 587 BCE, preserve the Hebrew of the last days of the kingdom.</p><p>Hebrew was first written in the Paleo-Hebrew alphabet, a form of the Phoenician alphabet of twenty-two letters. After the Babylonian exile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> gradually adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> script, known as the square script, which became the Hebrew alphabet used ever since; the older letters survived for a time on coins and in the Samaritan script.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="structure">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>, Hebrew builds words from roots of usually three consonants, combined with patterns of vowels and affixes. From the root s-p-r come sefer, “book”, sofer, “scribe”, sippur, “story”, and mispar, “number”. Verbs are formed in a system of patterns, the binyanim, which express active, passive, causative, intensive and reflexive meanings of the same root.</p><p>Biblical Hebrew had a distinctive verbal system, in which narratives were told in a form combining the conjunction “and” with a verb form, and its usual word order placed the verb before the subject. Hebrew has two genders, masculine and feminine, which mark verbs as well as nouns and adjectives, and a dual for some pairs, such as yadayim, “hands”.</p><p>The Hebrew alphabet of twenty-two letters is written from right to left and originally recorded only consonants. Some letters came to be used for long vowels, and between the 8th and 10th centuries CE the scholars known as the Masoretes, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a>, created a complete system of vowel points and accent marks, to preserve the exact reading of the Bible as it was chanted in the synagogue. Modern Hebrew is usually written without vowel points, except in poetry, children’s books and texts for learners.</p><p>Modern Hebrew has kept the morphology of the ancient language, while its sound system and syntax have been shaped by the languages of its first modern speakers, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a> and other European languages, and by Arabic: its standard pronunciation follows the Sephardi tradition, its word order is usually subject, verb and object, and it has a simple system of past, present and future tenses.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="speakers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Speakers &amp; Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/babylon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylon.jpg" alt="Babylon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ishtar_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Stanley, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hebrew was the language of the kingdoms of Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> until their conquest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> in 722 BCE and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 587 or 586 BCE. During and after the Babylonian exile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, the common language of the Persian empire, became the everyday language of many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, but Hebrew continued to be spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> for centuries. The Dead Sea Scrolls, from the 3rd century BCE to the 1st century CE, are mostly in Hebrew, and letters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Simon bar Kokhba</a>, the leader of the revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 132 to 135 CE, were written in Hebrew as well as in Aramaic and Greek.</p><p>Mishnaic Hebrew, the language of the Mishnah, the code of Jewish law compiled around 200 CE, reflects a spoken language that differed from Biblical Hebrew. By the 3rd and 4th centuries, however, Hebrew had ceased to be anyone’s mother tongue, and Jews spoke Aramaic, Greek and later Arabic, Persian, the Judaeo-Spanish known as Ladino and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a>, among other languages.</p><p>For the next sixteen centuries Hebrew was not a dead language but a language of learning and worship, leshon ha-kodesh, “the holy tongue”. Jews prayed, studied, wrote law, commentary, philosophy, science and poetry in it, corresponded in it across the diaspora, and taught it to their children, who could read it but did not speak it at home.</p><p>In the 18th and 19th centuries, writers of the Jewish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> used Hebrew for modern literature and journalism, and the Zionist movement made its revival as a spoken language a national cause. Eliezer Ben-Yehuda, who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1881, insisted on speaking only Hebrew at home and compiled a great dictionary; teachers in the new Jewish settlements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> taught in Hebrew, and the language spread with the schools. In 1913 a dispute over the language of instruction at the new Technion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> ended in victory for Hebrew. The British Mandate recognised Hebrew as an official language in 1922, and in 1948 it became the main language of the new State of Israel, where immigrants from around the world learned it in intensive courses. Today it is spoken by some 9 million people.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="literature">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Literature &amp; Learning</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/isaiah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaiah.jpg" alt="Isaiah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jesaja_(Michelangelo).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Michelangelo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, the Tanakh, is the foundational work of Hebrew literature: the Torah, the historical and prophetic books, the Psalms, the wisdom of Proverbs and Job, and the poetry of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a>, composed and edited over many centuries and reaching its final form in the last centuries BCE. Its oldest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> are among the Dead Sea Scrolls, found from 1947 near Qumran, such as the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> Scroll, copied around 125 BCE.</p><p>Rabbinic literature continued in Hebrew: the Mishnah, the commentaries of the Midrash, and the prayers of the synagogue, while the Talmud mixed Hebrew with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>. From the 6th century, poets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> composed liturgical poems, piyyutim, of great complexity. The Masoretes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> fixed the text of the Bible with its vowels and accents; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> Codex, written around 930, is the most famous of their manuscripts.</p><p>In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Jewish poets writing in Hebrew under the influence of Arabic poetry created a golden age of Hebrew verse, both sacred and secular: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-ibn-naghrela">Samuel ha-Nagid</a>, Solomon ibn Gabirol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> Halevi, whose “Songs of Zion” express longing for the Land of Israel. Scholars such as Rashi, in 11th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, wrote commentaries on the Bible and Talmud still studied today, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> wrote his great code of Jewish law, the Mishneh Torah, in clear Hebrew in the 12th century.</p><p>Modern Hebrew literature began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>; Abraham Mapu’s The Love of Zion, of 1853, is considered the first Hebrew novel. Hayim Nahman Bialik became the national poet of the Hebrew revival, Shmuel Yosef Agnon won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1966, and writers such as Yehuda Amichai, Amos Oz and David Grossman have made Israeli literature known around the world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="change">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Change &amp; Descendants</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hebrew has passed through several stages: Biblical Hebrew, itself changing between the earliest poems and the latest books; the Hebrew of the Dead Sea Scrolls; Mishnaic Hebrew, with a new vocabulary and a present tense formed from the participle; Medieval Hebrew, enriched by translators, such as the Ibn Tibbon family of Provence, who created a philosophical and scientific vocabulary modelled on Arabic; and Modern Hebrew.</p><p>The revival required thousands of new words for modern life. Ben-Yehuda and his successors coined them from ancient roots, coining milon, “dictionary”, from mila, “word”, and later bodies did the same: the Hebrew Language Committee, founded in 1890, and its successor, the Academy of the Hebrew Language, founded in 1953, which continues to propose new terms; maḥshev, “computer”, for example, was formed from the root of “thinking” and “calculating”. Modern Hebrew has also borrowed many international words and absorbed expressions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a>, Arabic and English.</p><p>Through translations of the Bible, Hebrew words have entered languages around the world: English has amen, hallelujah, sabbath, cherub, jubilee and messiah, and many biblical expressions. The Hebrew alphabet was also used to write Yiddish, Ladino, Judaeo-Arabic and other Jewish languages.</p><p>In 2018 a law defined Hebrew as the official language of Israel, giving Arabic, previously also official, a special status.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible.jpg" alt="Hebrew Bible" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tanach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LGLou, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hebrew is the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, one of the most influential texts in human history, sacred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and to Christians as the Old Testament, and revered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Its stories, laws, prophecies and poems, read in translation across the world, have shaped religion, law, ethics, art and literature, and the study of the Hebrew original has been part of Christian as well as Jewish scholarship since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><p>For two thousand years Hebrew sustained the unity of Jewish communities scattered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, who shared its prayers, texts and learning even when they spoke different languages.</p><p>Its revival as a spoken language is unique in history. No other language that had ceased to be anyone’s mother tongue has become the everyday language of a nation, and the Hebrew revival has inspired movements to revitalise endangered languages elsewhere.</p><p>Today Hebrew is the language of a vibrant modern culture of literature, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, music, film, science and technology in Israel, and it continues to be studied around the world as the key to the Bible and to three thousand years of Jewish writing.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Hebrew, the Canaanite language of ancient Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, is the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>. Replaced in daily speech by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and other languages, it remained for two thousand years the language of Jewish prayer, law, poetry and learning, and was revived as a spoken language from the late 19th century to become the language of Israel, spoken by some 9 million people.</em></p>

